      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the perimeter of the larger triangle, we first need to determine the scale factor between the two similar triangles. Since the longest side of the original triangle is 10 cm, and the longest side of the larger triangle is 25 cm, we can calculate the scale factor as follows:\n\nScale factor = (Longest side of larger triangle) / (Longest side of original triangle)\nScale factor = 25 cm / 10 cm\nScale factor = 2.5\n\nNow that we have the scale factor, we can find the lengths of the other two sides of the larger triangle. Since the original triangle is isosceles, both of the equal sides are 8 cm long. To find the length of the third side, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\nThird side^2 = (Longest side of original triangle)^2 - (Equal side)^2\nThird side^2 = (10 cm)^2 - (8 cm)^2\nThird side^2 = 100 cm^2 - 64 cm^2\nThird side^2 = 36 cm^2\nThird side = √36 cm^2\nThird side = 6 cm\n\nNow that we have the lengths of all three sides of the larger triangle, we can find its perimeter:\n\nPerimeter = (Longest side of larger triangle) + (Equal side) + (Third side)\nPerimeter = 25 cm + 8 cm + 6 cm\nPerimeter = 39 cm\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the larger triangle is 39 centimeters.",
