      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand the concept of similar triangles. Two triangles are similar if their corresponding angles are equal and their corresponding sides are proportional. In this case, we are given an isosceles triangle with side lengths 8 cm, 8 cm, and 10 cm. We are also given that the longest side of a similar triangle is 25 cm. We need to find the perimeter of this larger triangle.\n\nStep 1: Identify the corresponding sides of the similar triangles.\nIn the original isosceles triangle, the longest side is 10 cm. In the similar triangle, the longest side is 25 cm. The ratio of the longest sides is:\n\n25 cm / 10 cm = 5/2\n\nStep 2: Determine the scale factor.\nThe scale factor is the ratio of the lengths of corresponding sides of similar triangles. In this case, the scale factor is 5/2.\n\nStep 3: Find the lengths of the other sides of the similar triangle.\nSince the original triangle is isosceles, the other two sides are equal. Let's call the length of each of these sides \"x\". Using the scale factor, we can set up the following equation:\n\nx * (5/2) = 10 cm\n\nSolving for x, we get:\n\nx = 10 cm * (2/5) = 4 cm\n\nSo, the other two sides of the similar triangle are each 4 cm long.\n\nStep 4: Calculate the perimeter of the larger triangle.\nThe perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of its sides. In this case, the perimeter is:\n\n4 cm + 4 cm + 25 cm = 33 cm\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the larger triangle is 33 cm.",
